      "Welcome to Spanish foreign policy and diplomacy. Everything here traces back to a single historical motivation genuinely different from the other countries covered in this course: Spanish foreign policy since the 1978 transition to democracy has centered on consolidating that democratic legitimacy through EU and NATO membership, not preventing renewed aggression the way German multilateralism does, and not projecting an independent great-power tradition the way French Gaullism does, but proving Spain had genuinely rejoined the community of democracies after decades of Franco-era isolation. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real Spanish foreign-policy decision actually encounters them. We start with EU and NATO membership as democratic consolidation, the genuinely gradual, contested path Spain actually took into NATO's full military structure, and the bilateral US defense relationship that predates NATO membership itself. We then turn to Spain's broader EU integration enthusiasm, and to the distinctive Ibero-American relationship, reinforced through formal development-cooperation architecture and a genuinely original global diplomatic initiative, that gives Spain a global reach disproportionate to its size. We close with three geographic and historical entanglements most other European countries simply do not face: the Mediterranean and North African relationship Ceuta, Melilla, and frontline migration pressure all create, the long-running Gibraltar sovereignty dispute with the United Kingdom, and the still-unresolved legacy of Spain's own colonial administration of Western Sahara. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: is this a foreign-policy choice any mid-sized European democracy might make, or is it one only Spain's own specific history and geography could actually produce.",
