          "Start with the foundational post-transition achievement everything else in this lecture builds on. Spain joined NATO, under the North Atlantic Treaty [1], in 1982 during Leopoldo Calvo-Sotelo's centrist government, then joined the European Economic Community, the forerunner of today's EU, in 1986 alongside Portugal, after nearly a decade of accession negotiations that began almost as soon as the 1978 Constitution was ratified. Both memberships were understood domestically less as ordinary foreign-policy choices weighed purely on their individual merits than as ways to consolidate and internationally validate Spain's new democracy after nearly four decades of Franco-era dictatorship and diplomatic isolation, a country the rest of Western Europe had spent a generation treating as an authoritarian outlier rather than a genuine democratic peer. NATO membership, unlike EEC membership, proved genuinely controversial at home: the opposition Socialist party, PSOE, campaigned against it ahead of the 1982 accession, and the question was ultimately put to a national referendum in March 1986, three years after PSOE itself had taken office and reversed its own prior opposition, with voters asked specifically whether to remain in the alliance on terms that excluded full integration into NATO's unified military command structure, the precise limitation the next concept in this lecture examines directly.",
